(Every json export, called the way you would actually use it: a realistic
 document read in, its values taken out one by one, the shape changed, and
 the whole thing written back out and read again to prove the two
 directions really are each other's inverse.

 This demo is the library's test - its output is compared byte for byte
 against json_demo.expected - so it is also the honest documentation of
 what each export does, edges included.

 One thing to know before reading the JSON below. A brace in a Vox text
 begins a format hole, so a literal brace is written twice: `{{` for `{`
 and `}}` for `}`. That doubling is Vox's rule about its own texts, not
 anything to do with JSON - the document that reaches the library has
 single braces, exactly as written on the wire.

   vox json.vox --shared -o libjson.so
   vox json_demo.vox --link json -o json_demo
   ./json_demo)

see json version "0.1" from "./libjson.lib".

(A document with one of everything in it: nested objects, arrays, an
 escape of every kind, a null, both booleans, an integer, a decimal, and
 an exponent. It is written across several lines, with the indentation a
 person would use, because JSON allows whitespace between any two tokens
 and the reader has to take it in its stride.)
Create a buffer called 'the document'.
append "{{\n" to 'the document'.
append "  \"name\": \"Ada Lovelace\",\n" to 'the document'.
append "  \"born\": 1815,\n" to 'the document'.
append "  \"score\": 99.5,\n" to 'the document'.
append "  \"active\": true,\n" to 'the document'.
append "  \"retired\": false,\n" to 'the document'.
append "  \"nickname\": null,\n" to 'the document'.
append "  \"tags\": [\"mathematician\", \"programmer\"],\n" to 'the document'.
append "  \"address\": {{ \"city\": \"London\", \"postcode\": \"W1\" }},\n" to 'the document'.
append "  \"notes\": \"she said \\\"hello\\\" \\\\ then a tab\\there\",\n" to 'the document'.
append "  \"nothing here\": [],\n" to 'the document'.
append "  \"nor here\": {{}},\n" to 'the document'.
append "  \"measurements\": [1, 2.5, -3, 1e2]\n" to 'the document'.
append "}}\n" to 'the document'.
a text called 'the source' is "{'the document'}".

Print "--- the document as it arrived ---".
Print 'the source'.

(Ask before you read, when a malformed document and the document `null`
 would mean different things to you.)
Print "--- reading it ---".
a boolean called 'it reads as json' is 'reads as json' of 'the source'.
Print "reads as json: {'it reads as json'}".

a value called held is 'from json' of 'the source'.
a map called person is held.
Print "entries: {person's length}".

(Every kind of value comes back as the Vox type that matches it, so an
 ordinary declaration is all it takes to get at one.)
Print "--- the values, one by one ---".
a text called name is person's "name".
Print "name: {name}".
a number called born is person's "born".
Print "born: {born}, and a century later: {born add 100}".
a float called score is person's "score".
Print "score: {score}, doubled: {score multiply 2.0}".
a boolean called active is person's "active".
Print "active: {active}".
a boolean called retired is person's "retired".
Print "retired: {retired}".
a value called nickname is person's "nickname".
If nickname is nothing then, Print "nickname: none was given".
a text called notes is person's "notes".
Print "notes: {notes}".

(A null and a key that was never there are different things, and stay
 different: the null is present and holds nothing.)
a value called 'a key that was never there' is person's "no such key".
On error Print "no such key: absent, which is not the same as null".

(Nested collections are ordinary lists and maps too.)
a list called tags is person's "tags".
Print "tags: {tags's length} of them, {tags}".
a value called 'the first tag' is element 1 of tags.
Print "the first tag: {'the first tag'}".
a map called address is person's "address".
a text called city is address's "city".
a text called postcode is address's "postcode".
Print "city: {city}, postcode: {postcode}".
a list called 'nothing here' is person's "nothing here".
Print "an empty array reads back with {'nothing here''s length} elements".
a map called 'nor here' is person's "nor here".
Print "an empty object reads back with {'nor here''s length} entries".

(Whether a number arrives as a number or as a decimal is decided by how it
 was written, not by what it happens to equal: 1 and -3 are integers, 2.5
 has a fraction, and 1e2 has an exponent, so it is a decimal that is worth
 exactly one hundred.)
a list called measurements is person's "measurements".
Print "measurements: {measurements}".
Print "measurements as json: {'to json' of measurements}".

(Changing the shape is changing an ordinary Vox map: the library hands you
 the real thing, not a view of it.)
Print "--- changing it ---".
Set person's "score" to 100.0.
Set person's "active" to false.
Set person's "nickname" to "the Enchantress of Number".
Set person's "children" to 3.
append "poet" to tags.
a float called 'the new score' is person's "score".
Print "score is now {'the new score'}, tags number {tags's length}".

Print "--- and writing it back out ---".
a text called rewritten is 'to json' of person.
Print rewritten.

(The proof that the two directions are inverses: read what was just
 written, and write that out again. If the second text differs from the
 first by so much as a byte, one of the two is wrong.)
Print "--- reading the new document back ---".
a value called 'read again' is 'from json' of rewritten.
a text called 'written twice' is 'to json' of 'read again'.
Print 'written twice'.
a boolean called 'the round trip held' is false.
If rewritten is 'written twice' then, set 'the round trip held' to true.
Print "the text survived a round trip: {'the round trip held'}".
a map called 'the person again' is 'read again'.
a text called 'the nickname again' is 'the person again''s "nickname".
a number called 'the children again' is 'the person again''s "children".
Print "and so did the values: {'the nickname again'} with {'the children again'} children".

(The same document, laid out for a person to read: every member on its own
 line, indented by that many spaces for each level it is inside, and a
 space after each colon. This is what json.dumps(indent=n) writes in
 Python, and it is the same document - it parses straight back.)
Print "--- and laid out to be read ---".
a text called 'laid out' is 'to json with indent' of person and 2.
Print 'laid out'.
a text called 'read back from the laid-out text' is 'to json' of {'from json' of 'laid out'}.
a boolean called 'the layout changed nothing' is false.
If rewritten is 'read back from the laid-out text' then, set 'the layout changed nothing' to true.
Print "and it says exactly what the compact one says: {'the layout changed nothing'}".
a text called 'an empty pair laid out' is 'to json with indent' of {{"here": {{}}, "none": []}} and 2.
Print "empty collections have no second line to go to:".
Print 'an empty pair laid out'.

(A document with no whitespace in it comes back out with none either: this
 library writes the tightest form there is, and the entries come out in
 the order the map holds them, which for a parsed document is the order
 they were written.)
Print "--- byte for byte, on a canonical document ---".
a text called canonical is "{{\"z\":1,\"a\":[true,false,null],\"m\":{{\"deep\":[[1],[2]]}}}}".
a text called 'canonical again' is 'to json' of {'from json' of canonical}.
Print canonical.
Print 'canonical again'.
a boolean called 'canonical survived' is false.
If canonical is 'canonical again' then, set 'canonical survived' to true.
Print "unchanged: {'canonical survived'}".

(Every kind of value, written from scratch. A boolean is spelt out, since
 Vox prints one as 1 and JSON does not.)
Print "--- writing each kind from scratch ---".
Print 'to json' of 42.
Print 'to json' of -7.
Print 'to json' of 0.
Print 'to json' of 3.25.
Print 'to json' of 2.0.
Print 'to json' of -0.5.
Print 'to json' of true.
Print 'to json' of false.
Print 'to json' of nothing.
Print 'to json' of "plain text".
Print 'to json' of "".
Print 'to json' of [].
Print 'to json' of {{}}.
Print 'to json' of [1, "two", 3.5, true, false, nothing, [1, 2]].

(Escapes, both directions. The seven characters JSON gives a letter of
 their own are written that way; every other control byte is written as
 \u00XX; a forward slash is left bare, which JSON allows; and the bytes of
 a letter above ASCII pass through untouched, so an accent and an emoji
 come out as themselves.)
Print "--- escapes ---".
Print 'to json' of "a quote \" a backslash \\ a tab \t a newline \n".
Print 'to json' of "a slash / stays bare".
Print 'to json' of "café and an emoji 😀".
Create a buffer called 'some control bytes'.
set byte 1 of 'some control bytes' to 1.
set byte 2 of 'some control bytes' to 7.
set byte 3 of 'some control bytes' to 31.
a text called 'the control bytes' is "{'some control bytes'}".
Print 'to json' of 'the control bytes'.
Print "and back again:".
Print 'from json' of "\"a quote \\\" a backslash \\\\ a tab \\t\"".
Print 'from json' of "\"\\u0041\\u00e9\\u4e2d\\ud83d\\ude00\"".
Print 'from json' of "\"a slash \\/ may be escaped on the way in\"".

(Numbers, in detail. An integer of eighteen digits or fewer is exact; a
 longer one becomes the nearest decimal, because that is the widest thing
 Vox can hold it in. An exponent moves the point rather than multiplying,
 so 1e2 is exactly a hundred.)
Print "--- numbers ---".
Print 'to json' of {'from json' of "999999999999999999"}.
Print 'to json' of {'from json' of "1234567890123456789012345"}.
Print 'to json' of {'from json' of "1e2"}.
Print 'to json' of {'from json' of "-1.5e3"}.
Print 'to json' of {'from json' of "1e-2"}.
Print 'to json' of {'from json' of "2.5E-2"}.
Print 'to json' of {'from json' of "-0"}.

(What is refused, and why it matters that it is refused rather than
 guessed at. Each of these is a document a looser reader would accept and
 hand you the wrong answer for.)
Print "--- malformed documents ---".
a boolean called 'cut short' is 'reads as json' of "{{\"a\": 1".
Print "cut short:            {'cut short'}".
a boolean called 'a trailing comma' is 'reads as json' of "[1, 2,]".
Print "trailing comma:       {'a trailing comma'}".
a boolean called 'an unquoted key' is 'reads as json' of "{{a: 1}}".
Print "unquoted key:         {'an unquoted key'}".
a boolean called 'single quotes' is 'reads as json' of "'a'".
Print "single quotes:        {'single quotes'}".
a boolean called 'a leading zero' is 'reads as json' of "01".
Print "leading zero:         {'a leading zero'}".
a boolean called 'a bare point' is 'reads as json' of "1.".
Print "a bare point:         {'a bare point'}".
a boolean called 'an unknown escape' is 'reads as json' of "\"a\\xb\"".
Print "an unknown escape:    {'an unknown escape'}".
a boolean called 'half a character' is 'reads as json' of "\"\\ud83d\"".
Print "half a character:     {'half a character'}".
a boolean called 'a raw newline inside' is 'reads as json' of "\"a\nb\"".
Print "a raw newline inside: {'a raw newline inside'}".
a boolean called 'left over text' is 'reads as json' of "1 2".
Print "left over text:       {'left over text'}".
a boolean called 'nothing at all' is 'reads as json' of "".
Print "nothing at all:       {'nothing at all'}".
a value called 'the malformed answer' is 'from json' of "{{\"a\": 1".
Print "and a malformed document answers: {'the malformed answer'}".

(Nesting is allowed 64 levels deep, in either direction. Deeper than that
 is refused rather than run at until the stack gives out - which is also
 what happens to a value that contains itself.)
Print "--- depth ---".
a boolean called 'ten deep' is 'reads as json' of "[[[[[[[[[[1]]]]]]]]]]".
Print "ten deep:      {'ten deep'}".
a map called 'a map that holds itself' is {{}}.
Set 'a map that holds itself's "self" to 'a map that holds itself'.
a text called 'the impossible text' is 'to json' of 'a map that holds itself'.
a buffer called 'how much came out' is "{'the impossible text'}".
Print "a map holding itself writes {'how much came out''s size} bytes, and the program is still here".
